gn and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=
=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h=
3>Even Grids</h3>=0D=0A<p>It=E2=80=99s always interesting observing multipl=
e people attack the same problem. Sometimes they aren=E2=80=99t even aware =
of each other. Like how Isaac Newton invented calculus in the mid 1660=E2=
=80=99s in England, then Gottfried Leibniz did the same a decade or so late=
r, independently (or so it is said).</p>=0D=0A<p>I just read Ibrahim Bendeb=
ka=E2=80=99s recent take on <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/Mq=
jBbPU2_IlY47Xf7PXZmQ~~/AABEfhA~/hPb4MmDnrIkiCGlkGySXzTyKj5mf1eg-Cv9ZT32ibQU=
f8HpWme0csop3AUu8aMVgfJo59gKZ23q-uSDuFBBnXvRw_vWygBxKZT0MjRr26DUXgUHBETysLm=
YW4mP7ENpbbj8mtiQNNc-vwkZJqchPGJxSIhxuH8dFAq2Araq3yTJX-NbrtiPqI5OLkjO6VDrG"=
>Flexbox Masonry Layout</a>. This approach is thick with mathematics, which=
 I cannot pretend to fully understand. I understand the requirements, thoug=
h, as it=E2=80=99s having a bunch of elements with <em>known aspect ratios<=
/em> laid out into rows that cleanly fill the horizontal space. You don=E2=
=80=99t get that behavior by default, so it requires using <code>aspect-rat=
io</code> that you calculate and then <em>also</em> apply to <code>flex-gro=
w</code>, so it requires understanding the flexbox model pretty deeply.</p>=
=0D=0A<img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com=
/cdn-cgi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/flexbox=
-model-demo.png" alt=3D"A flexbox layout of multicolor rectangles, demonstr=
ating the technique in Ibrahim Bendebka=E2=80=99s article. " width=3D"450"/=
>=0D=0A<p>Late last year, Ian Kilpatrick came across the same exact idea in=
 <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/TmvortLl2iKCYbo3JJGo1w~~/AABE=
fhA~/CDlR4iAhhEqJC7WbKLDexiTLFzYFlx54QjYUg5pX8kv474k9_bdXNRWCFnd5uMilaC0pnx=
FJN1FyOfYY-8i_Fj6tNlTylOKfFo4nwOYZXPZvoWIgFUE95-399qYtdkXrxE_2L-y5X2qlh0UCF=
h_aWw~~">Exploiting flex-grow for an Image Gallery</a>, and I really have n=
o idea whether Ibrahim and Ian know of each other. But in both cases, the a=
spect ratio is calculated and used. This is necessary to keep the elements =
of equal height and exploit how <code>flex-grow</code> works to get even ed=
ges.</p>=0D=0A<img src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenas=
sets.com/cdn-cgi/image/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark=
/flexgrow_image_gallery.jpg" alt=3D"Example photo grid using the techniques=
 from Ian Kilpatrick's article. " width=3D"450"/>=0D=0A<p>Ian <em>was</em> =
aware of Antin Harasymiv=E2=80=99s take in <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostm=
ail2.com/f/a/LmbSms9DYBWTZgH47aY8bg~~/AABEfhA~/pgWBgj-aGsmJGPyZfHwfL9akUkHw=
4hB830LpMjzf_xOyo_KaKcNDHbFmxmlneeCzqu-R0aawCBRdEmuQ08jiUuoxfjWtr-N6UHWRNNV=
4InDxN59UMZTS9dnm4atAONKqGX3_6ZNC2P0RjeuCH7_vD_xz4Iyi85lAnq9NT9omFKo~">Buil=
ding the Google Photos Web UI</a> from 2018, but it didn=E2=80=99t detail t=
he specific web-based layout solution to get there.</p>=0D=0A<img src=3D"ht=
tps://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cgi/image/width=
=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/google_photos_ui.png" alt=
=3D"The Google Photos UI displaying a photo grid behind a cellphone. " widt=
h=3D"450"/>=0D=0A<p>I=E2=80=99d stop there, because that=E2=80=99s already =
pretty interesting seeing three people very mentally invested in this exact=
 same problem. But of course there are more.</p>=0D=0A<ul>=0D=0A<li>(2019) =
Tim Van Damme worked out an <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/5F=
TsLLtByCxWL6MJLuJf8w~~/AABEfhA~/H5G3vtBKwtfMBUwDasjrmVQCS2M4fINFpCAxO2mzWtg=
poCOZ2p55eDdM2TmsaTS7NpRhlgFnauToOkLDCsyMwmwSOWlCpfEL-09Zm7VuZzAwpHFCpYaeA8=
p45z2M9kHwm5JBb2biImOU7abXBWYyCdpfoXKvI1XvYF2XAckQNak~">Adaptive Photo Layo=
ut with Flexbox</a> which is much simpler and very close to the same, just =
doesn=E2=80=99t match aspect ratios perfectly.</li>=0D=0A<li>(2022) Nils Bi=
nder wrote <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/TcjR0N3v1DqxHzeN-RJ=
v0w~~/AABEfhA~/SyDDvASKQeyQLAXgLCRGZNFa-jXJKbh8dBhBOY9G4runOW2j_rwj54PyPF0e=
9VB-E3QJrwe3rUbQ82b0nDKNh6dzLQErsA9xA5tM3gOgh003ZVxF9MKnMiRBwh1vlscfml8AEuQ=
b1QNP19Vk3ST00wxOPDjEFZ4D8bLYYxJifcOZ-GGh5jGfVL964ddPk8Wp">Building a combi=
ned CSS-aspect-ratio-grid</a> which lands in exactly the same place, starti=
ng with pre-calculated aspect ratios.</li>=0D=0A<li>(2015!) Kartik Prabhu h=
as probably the earliest take with <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com=
/f/a/D-qZKV0dK1YzD5A50aFaUA~~/AABEfhA~/p0HhRoO7cVk3qt7vW99JIqJF826sTxiq4GJk=
26hJ-LK4Fx05zhekbBUwocFV9eFDesBE6WqUM8cRbhB8nA-Vzu9OLBgr0fgQDAX4tOnnu7EMjgB=
OZ0EuG4T4Sp_UsgcThHwKTQxywf2G_KDtTBC57VrD5nKRKQ2XrZrwITwoz7Y~">Equal Height=
 Images with Flexbox</a>, literally latching onto <code>flex-grow</code> an=
d its <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/QYgolHNzBJincsOV1Mg6UQ~~=
/AABEfhA~/pa4V90kbk1DlAkfp44sUanIXHtTa84pUiIknp-vEEaHO6R8tU7HaBhecwOHgkRvyC=
3tyEwyvBrahO7qbcAouUAiDf03D65PFjaKFEcH5e1_J-hlpYtpjZo_RlceUOFYq36TZwOkoR_6d=
1eqqV-B4Ow~~">=E2=80=9Cweird=E2=80=9D</a> behavior.</li>=0D=0A</ul>=0D=0A<h=
r />=0D=0A<p>In a related vein, I enjoyed Rostislav Melkumyan=E2=80=99s tak=
e on <a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/GkSR-3tSkgOcq8QZxfUvNg~~/=
AABEfhA~/mISi56NNXUWbqwJFjZv65CLIl2bNs6H1XhgTPbTS_SLf06nJSxLEkztpTy4BAJkVyL=
rs3_I5SaIuIpeVohGKZq2383Klhyge_0P3ewrsdnWr01HE32K5hwolDGeQGzs4lCrvOIY7IjFFc=
2hq6TRUQQ~~">=E2=80=9CThe logo soup problem=E2=80=9D</a>. This problem also=
 has many solutions over many years. The idea is similar in that logos can =
have extremely different aspect ratios. And just plopping them into a grid =
without much thought it almost sure to make them look awkward.</p>=0D=0A<p>=
The solution presented here requires more work that just aspect ratio alone=
. There is also a scaling factor that needs to be tweaked to balance the l=
ogos, <em>as a start</em>. But each logo can have a different =E2=80=9Cvisu=
al weight=E2=80=9D which is really the thing that can throw a grid of logos=
 off, visually. Fortunately even that can be calculated with =E2=80=9Cpixel=
 density=E2=80=9D and used as additional scaling information.</p>=0D=0A<img=
 src=3D"https://production-codepen-email-assets.codepenassets.com/cdn-cgi/i=
mage/width=3D600,quality=3D90,format=3Dauto/codepen-spark/logo_soup_problem=
.png" alt=3D"A step in the solution to the logo soup problem, where each l=
ogo is shown with grid lines surrounding it. " width=3D"450"/>=0D=0A<hr />=
=0D=0A<p>These grids typically have something of a =E2=80=9Cmasonry=E2=80=
=9D feel to them in that the grid lines aren=E2=80=99t straight <em>both</e=
m> vertically and horizontally. Although, as weird as it is, =E2=80=9Cmason=
ry=E2=80=9D usually refers to straight vertical lines and staggered horizon=
tal ones (like a brick wall turned sideways). This has been the subject of =
much standards work over many years, but the answer to this style of layout=
 is called =E2=80=9C<a href=3D"https://go.sparkpostmail2.com/f/a/bCqnIkLows=
